\section{Make Connectivity Real}

Placement assigns cells to legal sites, and routing assigns resource paths that connect their terminals. In an FPGA, a packer first groups primitives into legal clusters; a site has a fixed set of available local connections, clock inputs and compatible primitives. Routing then claims programmable switches and tracks. In an ASIC, macros and I/O are floorplanned, standard cells are placed in rows, clock trees are built, and signal wires are routed subject to design rules. Other phases address power distribution, antenna effects, filler cells, and manufacturability. Two legal placements of the same mapped netlist can lead to very different route lengths and congestion.

The asynchronous CASH paper gives a useful staging example: its back end emits structural Verilog, the synthesis stage produces a gate-level circuit, and place-and-route yields layout and wire information. The updated timing characterization belongs to the physical stage, not to the source. In the Vienna architecture-description work, synthesizable HDL is one of several generated outputs. A cycle-accurate simulator from the same description is useful for checking the architecture, but does not supply the wires or package pins.

