\section{Take an iCEstick Example Through Device Configuration}

Assume the iCEstick evaluation board with its HX1K in the TQ144 package and 12 MHz oscillator. Save the following Verilog-2005 as \texttt{blink.v}; the register initialization depends on the target synthesis and configuration flow.

\begin{verbatim}
module blink(input clki, output led1);
    reg [23:0] q = 24'd0;
    always @(posedge clki) q <= q + 24'd1;
    assign led1 = q[23];
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

After \(n\) edges the counter is \(n\bmod2^{24}\), so the LED's drive changes every \(2^{23}\) edges and has a full period of \(2^{24}/12{,}000{,}000\approx1.398\) s. That is a functional calculation at the nominal input frequency, not the maximum clock rate. The PCF pin syntax below follows the \href{https://github.com/YosysHQ/nextpnr/blob/main/ice40/examples/blinky/blinky.pcf}{nextpnr iCEstick example}. Verify any substitute board against its schematic; a package name alone identifies no oscillator or LED.

\begin{verbatim}
set_io clki 21
set_io led1 99
\end{verbatim}

With those lines saved as \texttt{blink.pcf}, the native command-line flow is:

\begin{verbatim}
yosys -p 'read_verilog blink.v; synth_ice40 -top blink -json blink.json'
nextpnr-ice40 --hx1k --package tq144 --freq 12 \
  --pcf blink.pcf --json blink.json --asc blink.asc \
  --report blink_report.json
icepack blink.asc blink.bin
\end{verbatim}

The \href{https://github.com/YosysHQ/nextpnr/blob/main/docs/constraints.md}{nextpnr constraint documentation} describes the default frequency target and architecture-specific pin files. Physical-flow pseudocode separates the obligations:

\begin{verbatim}
require clock_and_pin_constraints_match_board()  # before routing
pack_and_place(mapped_cells)                     # legal clusters/sites
route_all_nets()                                 # connectivity first
review_timing_and_resource_reports()             # not only exit status
encode_routed_configuration()                    # separate from report
\end{verbatim}

