\section{Accept a Delivered Artifact Only with Its Checks}

The IceStorm binary encodes configuration for a particular device, rather than a program for a processor. Loading it into volatile configuration memory versus an external boot flash is a board-level decision. The report and logs are evidence to review, not payloads to program. They should identify utilization, routed timing and violated paths under the recorded constraints. A design can fit by total LUT count but fail to route around a congested region, or close an internal clock target while leaving significant I/O timing unchecked.

ASIC deliverables include signoff reports, extracted parasitics and layout such as GDSII (or OASIS), not an FPGA bitstream. Design-rule checks assess legal geometry; layout-versus-schematic compares extracted connectivity with the intended circuit. Neither is interchangeable with RTL equivalence, and none substitutes for setup and hold analysis. A physical result is acceptable only when all required nets are routed, the intended pins and clock constraints were read, utilization and congestion are within the project limits, and the routed min/max timing reports cover the intended endpoints. Routability and timing heuristics are not proofs of optimality. For a meaningful comparison, hold the target, constraints and reporting stage fixed and record tool versions and seeds. Fix real congestion or interface assumptions rather than hiding a failure by changing what a report checks.
